'''Crater Lake''' ([[Klamath language|Klamath]]: {{lang|kla|Giiwas}})<ref name="giiwas">{{cite web archived|title=Klamath Tribes Language Project|publisher=The Klamath Tribes|year=2012|url=http://www.klamathtribes.org/vocabulary.html|access-date=March 21, 2016|archive-date=August 20, 2013|url-status=dead}}</ref> is a [[volcanic crater lake]] in south-central [[Oregon]] in the [[Western United States|Western]] United States. It is the main feature of [[Crater Lake National Park]] and is a tourist attraction known for its deep blue color and [[water clarity]]. The lake partly fills a {{convert|2148|ft|m|adj=mid|-deep}} [[caldera]]<ref name="facl">{{cite web
  | title = Facts about Crater Lake
  | work = Oregon Explorer
  | publisher = [[Oregon State University]]
  | url = http://oe.oregonexplorer.info/craterlake/facts.html
  | access-date = 2009-02-05
  | archive-date = March 22, 2012
  | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20120322145409/http://oe.oregonexplorer.info/craterlake/facts.html
  | url-status = live
  }}</ref> that was formed around 7,700 (± 150) years ago<ref name="SI_GVP ">
  {{cite gvp
  | vn = 322160
  | name = Crater Lake
  | access-date = 2008-12-18}}</ref>
by the collapse of the volcano [[Mount Mazama]]. No rivers flow into or out of the lake; the evaporation is compensated for by rain and snowfall at a rate such that the total amount of water is replaced every 150 years.<ref>{{Cite book |last=Cranson |first=K. R. |title=Crater Lake, gem of the Cascades: the geological story of Crater Lake National Park, Oregon |date=2005 |publisher=KRC Press |isbn=978-0-9770880-0-3 |edition=3rd |location=Lansing, Mich |page=120 |quote=A drop of water entering Crater Lake will remain there for about 150 years, its so-called residence time.}}</ref> With a depth of {{convert|1949|ft|m}},<ref name=":0">{{cite journal|last1=Bacon|first1=CR|last2=Gardner|first2=JV|last3=Mayer|first3=LA|last4=Buktnenica|first4=MW|last5=Dartnell|first5=P|last6=Ramsey|first6=DW|last7=Robinson|first7=JE|title=Morphology, volcanism, and mass wasting in Crater Lake, Oregon|journal=GSA Bulletin|date=June 2002|volume=114|issue=6|pages=675–692|url=http://ccom.unh.edu/sites/default/files/publications/Bacon%20et%20al.%202002.pdf|display-authors=4|doi=10.1130/0016-7606(2002)114<0675:mvamwi>2.0.co;2|bibcode=2002GSAB..114..675B|access-date=May 6, 2016|archive-date=September 19, 2015|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20150919070622/http://ccom.unh.edu/sites/default/files/publications/Bacon%20et%20al.%202002.pdf|url-status=live}}</ref> the lake is the [[List of lakes by depth|deepest]] in the United States. In the world, it ranks eleventh for maximum depth, as well as fifth for mean depth.

Crater Lake features two small islands. [[Wizard Island]], located near the western shore of the lake, is a [[cinder cone]] about {{convert|316|acre|ha|abbr=off}} in size. [[Phantom Ship (island)|Phantom Ship]], a natural rock pillar, is located near the southern shore.

Since 2002, one of Oregon's regular-issue [[Vehicle registration plates of Oregon|license-plate design]] has featured Crater Lake<ref>{{cite news|last=Goetze|first=Janet |date=August 26, 2002 |title=Crater Lake plates aglow with color|newspaper=The Oregonian|location=Portland, Oregon|page=B4}}</ref> and a one-time plate surcharge is used to support the operation of Crater Lake National Park.<ref>{{cite news |last=Killen |first=John |date=November 20, 2014 |title=Oregon license plates: Something for just about everyone |newspaper=The Oregonian |location=Portland, Oregon |url=https://www.oregonlive.com/multimedia/2014/11/oregon_license_plates.html |url-status=live |access-date=May 19, 2019 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191222225324/https://www.oregonlive.com/multimedia/2014/11/oregon_license_plates.html |archive-date=December 22, 2019}}</ref> The commemorative Oregon [[State Quarters|State Quarter]], which was released by the [[United States Mint]] in 2005, features an [[:File:Oregon quarter, reverse side, 2005.jpg|image of Crater Lake]] on its reverse.<ref>
  {{cite web
 |title=The Oregon Quarter: The United States Mint
 |publisher=U.S. Department of the Treasury
 |url=http://www.usmint.gov/historianscorner/?action=coinDetail&id=29113
 |access-date=2007-06-29
 |url-status=dead
 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20090927174530/http://www.usmint.gov/historianscorner/?action=coinDetail&id=29113
 |archive-date=2009-09-27
}}</ref>

The lake and surrounding park areas offer many recreational activities, including hiking, biking, snowshoeing, fishing, and cross-country skiing, and during the summer, campgrounds and lodges at Crater Lake are open to visitors.

== Location ==
Crater Lake is in [[Klamath County, Oregon|Klamath County]], around {{convert|60|mi|km}} northwest of the county seat of [[Klamath Falls, Oregon|Klamath Falls]], and about {{convert|80|mi|km}} northeast of the city of [[Medford, Oregon|Medford]].<ref name="us-park-service-directions">{{cite web|publisher=National Park Service|url=https://www.nps.gov/crla/planyourvisit/directions.htm|title=Crater Lake National Park: Directions|access-date=2009-12-10|archive-date=October 18, 2018|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20181018122408/https://www.nps.gov/crla/planyourvisit/directions.htm|url-status=live}}</ref>

A Native American connection with this area has been traced back to before the eruption of Mount Mazama. Archaeologists have found sandals and other artifacts buried under layers of ash, dust, and [[pumice]] that antedate the eruption roughly 7,700 years ago.<ref name=":6" /> Crater Lake remains significant to the Klamath tribes today.<ref name=":4" /> The Klamath name for the lake is ''Giiwas''.<ref name=":6" />

In June 1853, Isaac Skeeter, John Wesley Hillman, and another man were the first non-Native Americans to report sighting the lake, while on a mining trip; Skeeter named it "Deep Blue Lake", inspired by Hillman's description of the site. The lake was renamed at least three times, as Blue Lake, Lake Majesty, and finally Crater Lake.<ref>
  {{cite book
  | last=Tilden
  | first = Freeman
  | title = The National Parks
  | publisher = Alfred A. Knopf
  | location = New York
  | year = 1968 }}</ref><ref>{{cite journal|last=Runkel|first=H.J.|title=Crater Lake Discovery Centennial|url=http://www.nps.gov/archive/crla/notes/vol19b.htm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20081204232255/http://www.nps.gov/archive/crla/notes/vol19b.htm|archive-date=2008-12-04|journal=Nature Notes from Crater Lake National Park|volume=XIX|year=1953}}</ref>

