== Dimensions and depth ==
The lake is {{convert|5|by|6|mi}} across, with a caldera rim ranging in elevation from {{convert|7000|to|8000|ft}} and an average lake depth of {{convert|1148|ft}}. The lake's maximum depth has been measured at {{convert|1949|ft}},<ref name="facl"/><ref name=":0" /><ref>{{cite web|title=Crater Lake (Mount Mazama)|url=http://www.skimountaineer.com/CascadeSki/CascadeSki.php?name=CraterLake|first=Amar|last=Andalkar|date=2003-12-18|work=Skiing the Cascade Volcanoes|publisher=skimountaineer.com|access-date=2013-07-08|archive-date=September 10, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130910021304/http://www.skimountaineer.com/CascadeSki/CascadeSki.php?name=CraterLake|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>{{cite web | first = Helen | last = Gibbons | title = CMG Maps Bottom of Crater Lake, Oregon | publisher = U.S. Geological Survey | date = September 2000 | url = https://soundwaves.usgs.gov/2000/09/fieldwork.html | access-date = 2010-05-16 | archive-date = May 28, 2010 | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20100528152129/http://soundwaves.usgs.gov/2000/09/fieldwork.html | url-status = dead }}</ref> which fluctuates slightly as the weather changes.<ref name="facl"/>  On the basis of maximum depth, Crater Lake is the deepest lake in the United States, the second-deepest in North America (after [[Great Slave Lake]] in Canada), and the [[List of lakes by depth|eleventh-deepest lake in the world]].<ref>{{cite encyclopedia|author1=Noah Tesch|title=9 of the World's Deepest Lakes|url=https://www.britannica.com/list/9-of-the-worlds-deepest-lakes|encyclopedia=Encyclopaedia Britannica|access-date=8 May 2017|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20170408154946/https://www.britannica.com/list/9-of-the-worlds-deepest-lakes|archive-date=8 April 2017|language=en|quote=#9 Crater Lake ({{convert|1,943|ft|m|abbr=off|sp=us|disp=sqbr}}), #8 Great Slave Lake ({{convert|2,015|ft|m|abbr=off|sp=us|disp=sqbr}})}}</ref> Crater Lake is often cited as the seventh-deepest lake in the world, but this ranking excludes [[Lake Vostok]] in Antarctica,<ref>{{cite web|url=http://novaonline.nvcc.edu/eli/evans/HIS241/Notes/Geography/Baikal.html|title=Lake Baikal|year=2007|first=C.T.|last=Evans|publisher=Northern Virginia Community College|access-date=2013-07-08|archive-date=July 24, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20130724051423/http://novaonline.nvcc.edu/eli/evans/HIS241/Notes/Geography/Baikal.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref name=herald>{{cite news|url=http://www.craterlakeinstitute.com/crater-lake-news/owen-crater-lake-depth.htm|title=Into the Deep: Crater Lake's ranking as one of the world's deepest lakes varies by how list is determined|first=Lee|last=Juillerat|date=2007-11-29|newspaper=Herald and News|location=Klamath Falls, Oregon|access-date=2013-07-08|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140817011210/http://www.craterlakeinstitute.com/crater-lake-news/owen-crater-lake-depth.htm|archive-date=2014-08-17|url-status=dead}}</ref> which is beneath about {{convert|13000|ft}} of ice, and the recent [[depth sounding]]s of [[O'Higgins/San Martín Lake]], which is along the border of [[Chile]] and [[Argentina]].<ref>{{cite news|url=http://projects.registerguard.com/csp/cms/sites/web/sports/outdoors/24516398-44/lake-park-kuehnert-snow-winter.csp|title=A snowshoe view: Take a ranger-guided winter trek around snowy Crater Lake|newspaper=The Register-Guard|location=Eugene, Oregon|page=D1|date=2010-03-16|format=Oregon Life section|first=Garret|last=Jaros|access-date=2013-07-08|archive-date=August 17, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140817105433/http://projects.registerguard.com/csp/cms/sites/web/sports/outdoors/24516398-44/lake-park-kuehnert-snow-winter.csp|url-status=live}}</ref>

When considering the mean, or average depth of lakes, Crater Lake becomes the deepest lake in the Western Hemisphere and the third-deepest in the world. Crater Lake Institute Director and [[Limnology|limnologist]] Owen Hoffman states that "Crater Lake is the deepest, when compared on the basis of average depth among lakes whose basins are entirely above [[sea level]]. The average depths of Lakes [[Lake Baikal|Baikal]] and [[Lake Tanganyika|Tanganyika]] are deeper than Crater Lake; however, both have basins that extend below sea level."<ref name=herald/><ref>{{cite web|title=Crater Lake, On Average, Is Deepest Lake in North America|date=2007-11-14|publisher=National Parks Traveler|first1=Kurt|last1=Repanshek|first2=Owen|last2=Hoffman|format=see story comments as well|url=http://www.nationalparkstraveler.com/2007/11/crater-lake-average-deepest-lake-north-america|access-date=2013-07-08|archive-date=August 14, 2014|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20140814095854/http://www.nationalparkstraveler.com/2007/11/crater-lake-average-deepest-lake-north-america|url-status=live}}</ref>

== Geology ==
{{Main|Mount Mazama}}
[[Image:Geologic map of Crater Lake floor.png|thumb|right|260px|Geologic map of the lake floor]]
[[File:Crater Lake, Oregon by Planet Labs.jpg|thumb|Crater Lake from space]]
Mount Mazama, part of the [[Cascade Range]] [[volcanic arc]], was built up mostly of [[andesite]], [[dacite]], and [[rhyodacite]] over a period of at least 400,000 years. The caldera was created in a massive [[volcanic eruption]] between 6,000 and 8,000 years ago that led to the subsidence of Mount Mazama. About {{convert|50|km3|mi3|order=flip}} of rhyodacite was erupted in this event. Since that time, all eruptions on Mazama have been confined to the caldera.<ref>{{cite web|url=https://vulcan.wr.usgs.gov/Volcanoes/CraterLake/description_crater_lake.html|publisher=U.S. Geological Survey|work=Cascades Volcano Observatory|title=Mount Mazama Volcano and Crater Lake Caldera, Oregon|first=Lyn|last=Topinka|date=2008-04-18|access-date=2013-07-08|archive-date=October 2, 2013|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20131002150147/http://vulcan.wr.usgs.gov/Volcanoes/CraterLake/description_crater_lake.html|url-status=live}}</ref><ref>Jewell and McRae, p. 571</ref>

Lava eruptions later created a central platform, [[Wizard Island]], Merriam Cone, and other, smaller volcanic features, including a rhyodacite dome that was eventually created atop the central platform. Sediments and [[landslide]] debris also covered the caldera floor.<ref>{{cite journal|author-link=Charles R. Bacon|first=Charles R|last=Bacon|author2=James V. Gardner |author3=Larry A. Mayer |author4=Mark W. Buktenica |author5=Peter Dartnell |author6=David W. Ramsey |author7=Joel E. Robinson |year=2002|title=Morphology, volcanism, and mass wasting in Crater Lake, Oregon|journal=[[Geological Society of America Bulletin]]|volume=114|pages=675–692|doi=10.1130/0016-7606(2002)114<0675:MVAMWI>2.0.CO;2|issue=6|bibcode=2002GSAB..114..675B}}</ref>

Eventually, the caldera cooled, allowing rain and snow to accumulate and form a lake. Landslides from the caldera rim thereafter formed debris fans and [[turbidite]] sediments on the lake bed. [[Fumarole]]s and [[hot spring]]s remained common and active during this period. Also after some time, the slopes of the lake's caldera rim more or less stabilized, streams restored a radial drainage pattern on the mountain, and dense forests began to revegetate the barren landscape. It is estimated that around 720 years was required to fill the lake to its present depth of {{convert|594|m|ft|order=flip}}. Much of this occurred during a period when the prevailing climate was less moist than at present.<ref>{{cite journal|first=Manuel|last=Nathenson|author2=Charles R. Bacon|author3=David W. Ramsey|year=2007|title=Subaqueous geology and a filling model for Crater Lake, Oregon|journal=Hydrobiologia|volume=574|issue=1 |pages=13–27|doi=10.1007/s10750-006-0343-5|bibcode=2007HyBio.574...13N |s2cid=39578681|url=https://zenodo.org/record/1232804|access-date=June 27, 2019|archive-date=December 29, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20191229023225/https://zenodo.org/record/1232804|url-status=live}}</ref>

Some [[hydrothermal vent|hydrothermal]] activity remains along the lake floor, suggesting that at some time in the future, Mazama may erupt once again.<ref>{{cite web
  | title = Geologic History of Crater Lake
  | work = Oregon Explorer
  | publisher = [[Oregon State University]]
  | url = http://oe.oregonexplorer.info/craterlake/geology.html
  | access-date = 2009-02-05
  | archive-date = August 23, 2011
  | archive-url = https://web.archive.org/web/20110823141106/http://oe.oregonexplorer.info/craterlake/geology.html
  | url-status = live
  }}</ref>

== Climate ==

Crater Lake features a [[subalpine climate]], with the rare dry-summer type ([[Köppen climate classification|Köppen classification]] ''Dsc'') owing to its high elevation and – like all of Oregon – the strong summer influence of the [[North Pacific High]]. In the summer, the weather is mild and dry, but in the winter is cold and the powerful influence of the [[Aleutian Low]] allows for enormous snowfalls averaging {{convert|463.1|in|m|2}} per year and maximum snow cover averaging {{convert|139|in|m|2|disp=or|abbr=on}}. This snow does not usually melt until mid-July, and allows for substantial glaciers on adjacent mountains. In the winter of 1949/1950 as much as {{convert|885.1|in|m|2}} of snow fell, while the less complete snow cover records show cover as high as {{convert|192|in|m|2|disp=or|abbr=on}} occurred during another particularly unsettled winter in 1981/1982.<ref>[[National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration]]; Climate at a Glance: [http://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/cag/time-series/us/35/05/pcp/9/06/1895-2014?base_prd=true&firstbaseyear=1896&lastbaseyear=1967&trend=true&trend_base=10&firsttrendyear=1967&lasttrendyear=2014&filter=true&filterType=binomial Oregon Climate Division 5 (High Plateau) October to June Precipitation] {{Webarchive|url=https://web.archive.org/web/20160212071131/http://www.ncdc.noaa.gov/cag/time-series/us/35/05/pcp/9/06/1895-2014?base_prd=true&firstbaseyear=1896&lastbaseyear=1967&trend=true&trend_base=10&firsttrendyear=1967&lasttrendyear=2014&filter=true&filterType=binomial |date=February 12, 2016 }}</ref> The heaviest daily snowfall was {{convert|37.0|in|cm|1}}, which occurred as recently as February&nbsp;28, 1971; {{convert|20|in|cm|abbr=on}} or more in one storm has occurred in both June and September. Hard frost is possible even into the summer, and the average window for freezing temperatures is August 20 through July 10, while for measurable (≥{{convert|0.1|in|cm|disp=or|abbr=on}}) snowfall, September 28 through June 11.<ref name=NOWData /> Crater Lake's surface seldom freezes during winter months.  Its resistance to surface freezing is due to the underlying heat reservoir remaining from summer heating. Abnormally cold temperatures in early 1949 froze the lake completely for three months. Ice first appeared around the shoreline and gradually grew towards the center of the lake. After the surface was solid, snowfalls deposited four feet of snow on the two inches to one foot of ice. While the lake was frozen, several groups of park employees walked to Wizard Island. Newspaper articles give evidence that Crater Lake froze over at least twice in the 1920's. With the warming climate of southern Oregon, complete freezing of the lake is becoming less likely though thin skim ice occasionally forms small patches on the surface. In 1985, skim ice covered over half the lake's surface.

