==Ecology==
[[File:Crater Lake Panorama, Aug 2013.jpg|thumb|right|upright=1.69|Panorama of Crater Lake and Wizard Island]]

Since the collapse of Mount Mazama due to a volcanic eruption formed Crater Lake, no fish inhabited the lake until [[William Gladstone Steel]] decided to stock it in 1888 to allow for fishing.<ref name=":1">{{Cite web archived|url=http://www.craterlakeinstitute.com/natural-history/fish-in-craterlake.htm|title=Fish of Crater Lake National Park|website=www.craterlakeinstitute.com|access-date=2019-03-10|archive-date=July 15, 2018|url-status=dead}}</ref> Regular stocking continued until 1941, when it was evident that the fish could maintain a stable population without outside interference. Six species of fish were originally stocked, but only two species have survived: [[kokanee salmon]] and [[rainbow trout]], with the former being the more plentiful.<ref name=":1" /> Fishing in Crater Lake is promoted because the fish species are not indigenous to the lake.<ref name=":2">{{Cite web|url=https://www.nps.gov/crla/faqs.htm|title=Frequently Asked Questions – Crater Lake National Park (U.S. National Park Service)|last1=Lake|first1=Mailing Address: Crater Lake National Park PO Box 7 Crater|last2=Us|first2=OR 97604 Phone:594-3000 Contact|website=www.nps.gov|language=en|access-date=2019-03-10|archive-date=March 10, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190310192356/https://www.nps.gov/crla/faqs.htm|url-status=live}}</ref>

Crater Lake is also known for the "[[Old Man of the Lake]]", a full-sized tree which is now a log that has been bobbing vertically in the lake for over a century.<ref>{{cite journal|last1=Kartchnerand|first1=W.E.|last2=Doerr| first2=J.E. Jr. |title=Wind Currents In Crater Lake As Revealed By The Old Man Of The Lake|url=http://www.nps.gov/archive/crla/notes/vol11-3c.htm|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20080611203611/http://www.nps.gov/archive/crla/notes/vol11-3c.htm|archive-date=2008-06-11|journal=Nature Notes from Crater Lake National Park|volume=XI|date=September 1938|issue=3}}</ref><ref>{{cite news |url=http://www.cbsnews.com/news/on-the-trail-the-old-man-of-crater-lake/ |title=On the Trail The Old man of Crater Lake |work=[[CBS News]] |date=October 9, 2016 |format=Video |access-date=October 9, 2016 |archive-date=October 10, 2016 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20161010124938/http://www.cbsnews.com/news/on-the-trail-the-old-man-of-crater-lake/ |url-status=live }}</ref> The low temperature of the water has slowed the decomposition of the wood, hence its longevity.

In 1987, scientists sent a [[submersible]] down to the depths of Crater Lake to obtain more information about the geology at the bottom of the lake,<ref>{{Cite web|url=https://www.nps.gov/crla/learn/nature/underwater_exploration.htm|title=Underwater exploration in Crater Lake – Crater Lake National Park (U.S. National Park Service)|last1=Lake|first1=Mailing Address: Crater Lake National Park PO Box 7 Crater|last2=Us|first2=OR 97604 Phone:594-3000 Contact|website=www.nps.gov|language=en|access-date=2019-03-10|archive-date=June 11, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190611013948/https://www.nps.gov/crla/learn/nature/underwater_exploration.htm|url-status=live}}</ref> and inspect moss samples found in moss beds as deep as {{convert|600|ft}}.<ref name=":3">{{Cite web|url=https://www.cnet.com/news/diving-into-mysterious-crater-lake/|title=Diving into mysterious Crater Lake|last=Olsen|first=Stefanie|website=CNET|language=en|access-date=2019-03-10|archive-date=April 30, 2020|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20200430084025/https://www.cnet.com/news/diving-into-mysterious-crater-lake/|url-status=live}}</ref>

Due to several unique factors, mainly that the lake has no inlets or [[tributaries]], the waters of Crater Lake are some of the purest in the world because of the absence of pollutants. Clarity readings from a [[Secchi disk]] have consistently been measured as being {{convert|120|ft|m|abbr=on}}, which is very clear for any natural body of water.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Grable |first=Juliet |date=September 17, 2022 |title=Scientists work to keep Oregon's Crater Lake clear for years to come |url=https://www.opb.org/article/2022/09/17/crater-lake-oregon-clear-water-quality/ |access-date=2022-09-18 |website=OPB |language=en |archive-date=September 17, 2022 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20220917160021/https://www.opb.org/article/2022/09/17/crater-lake-oregon-clear-water-quality/ |url-status=live }}</ref> In 1997, scientists recorded a record clarity of {{convert|142|ft|m|abbr=on}}. However, in 2025, scientists recorded a clarity of {{convert|78|ft|m|abbr=on}}, which means the lake's water is getting cloudier and may be an indication of issues with the lake's physics, chemistry, and ecology.<ref>{{Cite web |last=Nuwer |first=Rachel |date=2025-11-14 |title=Mixing Is the Heartbeat of Deep Lakes. At Crater Lake, It's Slowing Down. |url=https://www.quantamagazine.org/mixing-is-the-heartbeat-of-deep-lakes-at-crater-lake-its-slowing-down-20251114/ |access-date=2025-11-17 |website=Quanta Magazine |language=en |archive-date=November 17, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20251117120753/https://www.quantamagazine.org/mixing-is-the-heartbeat-of-deep-lakes-at-crater-lake-its-slowing-down-20251114/ |url-status=live }}</ref>

The lake has relatively high levels of dissolved salts, total [[alkalinity]], and conductivity. The average [[pH]] has generally ranged between 7 and 8.<ref>
