== Sacred significance ==
[[File:Llao Rock (3679357909).jpg|right|thumb|upright|Llao Rock]]
The [[Klamath people|Klamath]] tribe of [[Native Americans in the United States|Native Americans]], whose oral history describes their ancestors witnessing the collapse of Mount Mazama and the formation of Crater Lake,<ref>{{Cite journal |last=Deur |first=Douglas |date=2002 |title=A Most Sacred Place: The Significance of Crater Lake among the Indians of Southern Oregon |url=https://pdxscholar.library.pdx.edu/anth_fac/79 |journal=Oregon Historical Quarterly |pages=19 |archive-date=August 10, 2024 |access-date=August 15, 2025 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20240810192707/https://pdxscholar.library.pdx.edu/anth_fac/79/ |url-status=live }}</ref> regard the lake as an "abode to the Great Spirit".<ref>Diller, J.S. (1898). Crater Lake, Oregon</ref> Klamath oral history tells of a battle between the sky god Skell and the god of the underworld [[Llao]] (a prominent feature at Crater Lake is Llao Rock). Mount Mazama was destroyed in the battle, creating Crater Lake, called ''giiwas'' in the [[Klamath language]].<ref name="giiwas" /> The Klamath people used Crater Lake in [[vision quest]]s, which often involved climbing the caldera walls and other dangerous tasks. Those who were successful in such quests were often regarded as having more spiritual powers. The tribe still holds Crater Lake in high regard as a spiritual site.<ref name=":6">{{cite web archived |title=Park History|publisher=National Park Service|url=http://www.nps.gov/crla/planyourvisit/upload/2010-history.pdf|archive-date=2013-04-15|access-date=2012-01-03|url-status=dead}}</ref><ref>{{cite web|title = Crater Lake as Sacred Site| publisher = Sacred Destinations| url = http://www.sacred-destinations.com/usa/crater-lake.htm| archive-url =https://web.archive.org/web/20090602231543/http://www.sacred-destinations.com/usa/crater-lake.htm| archive-date = 2009-06-02}}</ref>

== Recreation ==
Located {{convert|90|km|mi|order=flip}} north of the city of [[Klamath Falls, Oregon|Klamath Falls]] and {{convert|100|km|mi|order=flip}} northeast of [[Medford, Oregon|Medford]], Crater Lake can be reached from [[U.S. Route 97]] on the east, on the southwest by Highway 62, and on the northwest by Highway 138.<ref name=NPS_MAP>{{cite web |url=https://www.nps.gov/crla/planyourvisit/maps.htm |title=Crater Lake National Park Oregon - Maps |publisher=[[USDOI]], [[National Park Service]] |website=www.nps.gov |access-date=2021-02-19 |archive-date=March 18, 2021 |archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20210318225847/https://www.nps.gov/crla/planyourvisit/maps.htm |url-status=live }}</ref> Crater Lake and the remnants of Mount Mazama can be seen from Rim Drive, a {{convert|33|mi|km|adj=on}} road that surrounds the caldera,{{sfn|Harris|1988|pp=155–156}} which is the only part within the Crater Lake National Park where vehicles are permitted.<ref name=NPS_MAP/> The [[Garfield Peak (Oregon)#Garfield Peak trail|Garfield Peak Trail]], which runs {{convert|1.5|mi|km}} east from the [[Crater Lake Lodge]], offers views from {{convert|1900|ft|m}} above the lake's surface, with Mount Shasta visible {{convert|125|mi|km}} southward. Another trail runs for {{convert|2.5|mi|km}} from Rim Drive's eastern edge to Mount Scott, which offers views of central and southern Oregon such as the Three Sisters located {{convert|80|mi|km}} north of Mazama and [[Mount Thielsen]], also to the north. The Cleetwood Cove Trail leads for {{convert|1|mi|km}} down the northern flank of the caldera rim, eventually reaching Cleetwood Cove where boat trips run from late June or early July throughout the summer season to Wizard Island. Wizard Island can be climbed, offering views of Crater Lake.{{sfn|Harris|1988|pp=155–156}}

Swimming is permitted in Crater Lake, but the only legal and safe way to get to the shore is by following Cleetwood Cove Trail, and people can enter the water from there.<ref name=":4">{{Cite web|url=https://www.nps.gov/crla/faqs.htm|title=Frequently Asked Questions|work=Crater Lake National Park|publisher=U.S. National Park Service|access-date=2019-03-11|archive-date=March 10, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190310192356/https://www.nps.gov/crla/faqs.htm|url-status=live}}</ref> Other activities include fishing and a 2-hour boat tour around the lake provided by a Park Ranger from [[Crater Lake National Park]].<ref name=":5">{{Cite web|url=http://www.travelheals.com/top-6-must-dos-at-crater-lake/|title=Top 6 Must-Dos at Crater Lake National Park|date=2015-02-11|website=Travel Heals|language=en-US|access-date=2019-03-11|archive-date=March 10, 2019|archive-url=https://web.archive.org/web/20190310100420/http://www.travelheals.com/top-6-must-dos-at-crater-lake/|url-status=live}}</ref>

As of 2026, the Cleetwood Cove Trail and Marina are closed for a three-year [[National Park Service]] rehabilitation project. Accordingly, lakeshore access, swimming, and public boat tours are suspended through the 2028 summer season.<ref>{{cite web archived |title=Cleetwood Cove Trail and Marina Rehabilitation - Crater Lake National Park (U.S. National Park Service) |url=https://www.nps.gov/crla/planyourvisit/cleetwood-cove-trail-and-marina-rehabilitation.htm |website=www.nps.gov |publisher=[[National Park Service]] |access-date=19 February 2026 |archive-date=6 February 2026 |language=en}}</ref>

As the region lies within a national park area, collecting rocks within the vicinity is prohibited unless a permit is obtained.<ref>{{cite journal |last1=Geist |first1=Dennis |last2=Wolff |first2=John |last3=Harpp |first3=Karen |date=2017 |title=Field-trip guide to a volcanic transect of the Pacific Northwest: Scientific Investigations Report 2017–5022–M |journal=Scientific Investigations Report |publisher=U.S. Geological Survey |location=Reston, VA |doi=10.3133/sir20175022M |issn=2328-0328 |doi-access=free }}</ref>  The park's facilities lie at Rim Village, at the southern edge of the caldera.<ref name=NPS_MAP/> Lodging and camping facilities open during the summer season between May and October.<ref>{{cite web archived|url=https://www.nps.gov/crla/planyourvisit/upload/Lodging-and-Camping-Summer-508.pdf|title=Lodging & Camping (Summer)|publisher=[[National Park Service]]|date=October 2017|access-date=May 24, 2018|archive-date=May 25, 2018|url-status=dead}}</ref> No lodges, gas stations, or camping areas remain open from October through late May.<ref>{{cite web archived|url=https://www.nps.gov/crla/planyourvisit/upload/Lodging-and-Camping-Winter-508.pdf|title=Lodging & Camping (Winter)|publisher=[[National Park Service]]|date=October 2017|access-date=May 24, 2018|archive-date=May 25, 2018|url-status=dead}}</ref> Popular activities within Crater Lake National Park include [[cycling|biking]],<ref>{{cite web archived|url=https://www.nps.gov/crla/planyourvisit/upload/Bicycling-2012-Anne-Spillane-508.pdf|title=Crater Lake: Bicycling|publisher=[[National Park Service]]|date=July 2012|access-date=May 24, 2018|archive-date=May 25, 2018|url-status=dead}}</ref> fishing,<ref>{{cite web archived|url=https://www.nps.gov/crla/planyourvisit/upload/Fishing-2010-Anne-Spillane-508.pdf|title=Crater Lake: Fishing|publisher=[[National Park Service]]|date=February 2010|access-date=May 24, 2018|archive-date=May 25, 2018|url-status=dead}}</ref> [[cross-country skiing]], and [[snowshoe running|snowshoeing]].<ref>{{cite web archived|url=https://www.nps.gov/crla/planyourvisit/upload/Ski-and-Snowshoe-Rentals-508.pdf|title=Crater Lake: cross-Country Ski and Snowshoe Rentals|publisher=[[National Park Service]]|date=October 2017|access-date=May 24, 2018|archive-date=May 25, 2018|url-status=dead}}</ref>

